From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 13:50:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] canbus: Arm FDCAN transmit interrupts on demand

All three M_CAN/FDCAN backends enabled transmit-completion interrupt sources permanently.  Every successful frame therefore entered the ISR and woke the CAN routing task, even when software had already emptied its queue.  At typical Klipper traffic rates that paid interrupt and cooperative-scheduler overhead once per physical frame for no useful state transition.

Keep completion sources masked during normal transmission and arm them only when canhw_send() observes a full hardware FIFO.  Clear stale status first and perform an interrupt-masked arm-and-recheck sequence so capacity returning at the boundary is either used immediately or produces a future wake; neither case can lose progress.

The ISR clears completion status seen alongside any interrupt, but notifies the routing layer only when software had armed that source as a capacity waiter.  It masks the source before waking, restoring one notification per blocked interval instead of one per CAN frame.

Apply the same documented invariant to STM32, ATSAM, and ATSAMD implementations.  FIFO ordering, arbitration, frame contents, and public status responses remain unchanged.

Signed-off-by: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>

diff --git a/src/atsam/fdcan.c b/src/atsam/fdcan.c
index 80090cda..7434cf57 100644
--- a/src/atsam/fdcan.c
+++ b/src/atsam/fdcan.c
@@ -87,9 +87,31 @@ int
 canhw_send(struct canbus_msg *msg)
 {
     uint32_t txfqs = CANx->MCAN_TXFQS;
-    if (txfqs & MCAN_TXFQS_TFQF)
-        // No space in transmit fifo - wait for irq
-        return -1;
+    if (txfqs & MCAN_TXFQS_TFQF) {
+        /*
+         * A completion interrupt is useful only after software has found the
+         * FIFO full and needs a future capacity notification.  Leaving it
+         * enabled continuously interrupts and wakes the cooperative scheduler
+         * after every frame, including the common case where no message is
+         * waiting.  Arm it on demand while interrupts are masked, then re-read
+         * TFQF so a completion between the first check and the IE write can not
+         * become a lost wakeup.  Clear stale completion flags before arming;
+         * otherwise an old successful send could cause an immediate wake while
+         * the FIFO holding the current burst is still full.
+         */
+        irqstatus_t flag = irq_save();
+        CANx->MCAN_IR = FDCAN_IE_TC;
+        CANx->MCAN_IE |= FDCAN_IE_TC;
+        barrier();
+        txfqs = CANx->MCAN_TXFQS;
+        if (txfqs & MCAN_TXFQS_TFQF) {
+            irq_restore(flag);
+            return -1;
+        }
+        // Capacity already returned; this call can fill it without a wakeup.
+        CANx->MCAN_IE &= ~FDCAN_IE_TC;
+        irq_restore(flag);
+    }
 
     uint32_t w_index = ((txfqs & MCAN_TXFQS_TFQPI_Msk) >> MCAN_TXFQS_TFQPI_Pos);
     struct fdcan_fifo *txfifo = &MSG_RAM.TXFIFO[w_index];
@@ -238,9 +260,19 @@ CAN_IRQHandler(void)
         }
     }
     if (ir & FDCAN_IE_TC) {
-        // Tx
+        /*
+         * IR records completions even while their interrupt sources are
+         * masked, so a receive interrupt can observe and clear an unrelated
+         * transmit flag.  Notify only if canhw_send() actually armed the source
+         * while waiting for capacity.  Mask it before waking the task so one
+         * completion releases one waiter instead of restoring per-frame IRQs.
+         */
+        uint32_t tx_ie = CANx->MCAN_IE & FDCAN_IE_TC;
         CANx->MCAN_IR = FDCAN_IE_TC;
-        canbus_notify_tx();
+        if (tx_ie) {
+            CANx->MCAN_IE &= ~FDCAN_IE_TC;
+            canbus_notify_tx();
+        }
     }
     if (ir & MCAN_IR_BO) {
         /*
@@ -387,7 +419,7 @@ can_init(void)
     /*##-3- Configure Interrupts #################################*/
     armcm_enable_irq(CAN_IRQHandler, CANx_IRQn, 1);
     CANx->MCAN_ILE = MCAN_ILE_EINT0;
-    CANx->MCAN_IE = (MCAN_IE_RF0NE | FDCAN_IE_TC | MCAN_IE_BOE
+    CANx->MCAN_IE = (MCAN_IE_RF0NE | MCAN_IE_BOE
                      | MCAN_IE_PEDE | MCAN_IE_PEAE);
 }
 DECL_INIT(can_init);
